   109  | 512  | duplicates| 9.49  | 8.99   | 7.85   | 6.79   |
   110  
   111  **This is NOT warm-up/caching.** Warm-up would make later measurements faster,
   112  but here N=1 (single call per repeat) gives HIGHER per-call times than N=1000.
   113  
   114  The correct explanation: **timeit has fixed overhead per repeat** (function call
   115  setup, timer resolution, loop iteration). When N=1, this overhead is a large
   116  fraction of the total time. When N=1000, it's negligible per call. The per-call
   117  division (total/N) does not fully subtract this overhead, so N=1 **overestimates**
   118  the true per-call time.
   119  
   120  For insertion_sort, the means are nearly identical across N values (e.g.,
   121  size=32 duplicates: N=1: 8.75, N=10: 8.81, N=100: 8.78 μs), confirming the
   122  bias is only significant when the operation is fast relative to timer overhead.
